METHOD
In log-concave mode, the deterministic ratio generator constructs
positive integer log-concave interval profiles by choosing
nonincreasing successive rational ratios, then divides out their
common divisor.  In normalized mode, it instead chooses a decreasing
positive integer sequence a_t and puts m_t=(2t+1)a_t.  At each
half-level it retains only profiles whose old single-reserve margin
vector u is coefficientwise nonnegative.  It then tests the first-wall
payment for every admissible reduced next factor, using exact
arbitrary-precision integers.  Independently, it records whether

  (2t+3)m_t >= (2t+1)m_(t+1)

holds at every adjacent pair.

The first counterexample has K=3, q=1, r=0,

  m=(32,64,128,256,384,576,864,1296,
     1944,1944,1944,972,486,243),
  u=(0,243,1215,486),
  (T_1)_(0,*)=(0,-2,-3,-4).

Its consecutive first-wall block is

  m_6+m_7+m_8+m_9=6048,

whereas the negative transform load is

  2u_1+3u_2+4u_3=6075.

Thus the payment margin is exactly -27.  The successive ratios of m
are

  2,2,2,3/2,3/2,3/2,3/2,3/2,1,1,1/2,1/2,1/2,

so log concavity is immediate, and u is displayed nonnegative.
Therefore log concavity plus the old decreasing reserve does not
imply the first-wall payment.

Normalized-dimension monotonicity plus the old reserve also does not
imply payment.  The normalized-only run gives, at K=5, q=1, r=0,

  m=(19,54,85,119,153,176,195,210,221,228,
     252,276,300,297,290,279,132,105,74),
  u=(3,7,11,147,101,31),
  (T_1)_(0,*)=(0,-2,-3,-4,-4,-4).

Here m_t/(2t+1) is the decreasing integer sequence

  (19,18,17,17,17,16,15,14,13,12,12,12,12,11,10,9,4,3,2),

but the wall block is 1125 and the negative transform load is 1163,
so the payment margin is exactly -38.  This profile is not log
concave.

The conjunction of log concavity, normalized-dimension monotonicity,
and u>=0 has no failure in 21,747,190 standard checks and 7,568,659
wide checks, for 29,315,849 exact payments in total.  This is bounded
coverage of this random-ratio generator, not evidence that the
three-condition implication is true.  The separate exhaustive
analyzer records the exact counterexample

  m=(1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,8,8,8,8),
  u=(0,0,0,8),
  K=3, q=1, r=0, payment=31-4(8)=-1.

Thus the random generator misses a decisive plateau-and-cutoff family.
For the same three-condition profiles, the analyzer also puts

  p_s=m_(L+s)-m_(2L-s-1)+m_(2L+s)

and checks both D_1(s)<=p_s and the stronger finite payment obtained
by replacing every D_1(s) in the nonnegative suffix-weight form by
p_s.  There are no cap failures in 10,629,331 checks and no finite
bound failures in 15,923,316 checks.  Replacing the exact
level-dependent suffix weights by their larger stable weights also
has no failure in those 15,923,316 cases.

The displayed deterministic witness shows why the stable audit must
retain u>=0.  At K=3, q=1, and r=0, the profile m_t=2t+1 on
0<=t<=19 is log concave and normalized-dimension decreasing, and its
three-image caps (19,25,31,37) are nonnegative.  But its reserve is
(-6,-6,-6,37), outside the admissible cone, and its stable margin is
64-2(25)-31-37=-54.  Thus the reserve is a necessary hypothesis for
the current stable target.  Log concavity without normalized-
dimension monotonicity has an exact cap failure in the wide run.
Normalized-dimension monotonicity without log concavity fails the
cap, the exact finite bound, and the stable bound, as the normalized-
only witnesses show.  These are bounded diagnostics for a three-term
decomposition, not proofs of either sublemma.  Since the exhaustive
witness satisfies all three scalar conditions, a proof must retain a
stronger invariant of the triangular-convolution factor-word origin.
The adaptive thread count reflects RAM limits and machine load and can
differ on replay; deterministic profiles and exact counts do not
depend on scheduling.

